<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rendlesham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rendlesham is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in the Deben valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> names it as a royal residence of the East Anglian kings, and excavations since 2008 have revealed a large high-status settlement of the 6th to 8th centuries with a great hall, a few kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a> burial ground.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rendlesham.jpg" alt="Rendlesham" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The National Churches Trust · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_of_England,_RENDLESHAM,_St_Gregory_the_Great_(31381227472).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
